<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">H. G. Wells</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Herbert George Wells</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herbert George <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> was an English writer, born in Bromley, who wrote more than forty novels and dozens of short stories besides works of social commentary and history. A student of biology under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley">Thomas Henry Huxley</a>, he made his name with science fiction: The Time Machine (1895), The Island of Doctor Moreau (1896), The Invisible Man (1897) and The War of the Worlds (1898) imagined time travel, alien invasion and invisibility before these were common in the genre. Novels such as Kipps (1905) and Tono-Bungay (1909) described English society. An outspoken socialist, he was for much of his adult life an advocate of eugenics, a view he had largely recanted by 1940. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/h-g-wells.jpg" alt="H. G. Wells" width="330" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Charles Beresford · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H.G._Wells_by_Beresford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Herbert George Wells was born on 21 September 1866 at Atlas House, 162 High Street, Bromley, Kent, and was called &quot;Bertie&quot; by his family. He was the fourth and last child of Joseph Wells, a shopkeeper and professional cricketer for Kent, and Sarah Neal, a former domestic servant.</p><p>In 1891 he married his cousin Isabel Mary Wells. They separated in 1894, when he fell in love with a student, Amy Catherine Robbins, with whom he moved to Woking in May 1895; they married in October of that year. His time at Woking was perhaps the most productive of his career, and there he planned and wrote The War of the Worlds and The Time Machine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His early novels, called &quot;scientific romances&quot;, invented several themes now classic in science fiction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> made them convincing by setting commonplace detail beside a single extraordinary assumption, a method dubbed &quot;Wells's law&quot;, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-conrad">Joseph Conrad</a> hailed him in 1898 as &quot;O Realist of the Fantastic!&quot;. He also wrote short stories such as &quot;The Country of the Blind&quot; (1904), realist novels such as The History of Mr Polly (1910), and in The World Set Free (1913) imagined an &quot;atomic bomb&quot; dropped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">aeroplanes</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> was a member of the Fabian Society and stood as a Labour Party candidate for London University in the 1922 and 1923 general elections. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1914, 1920 and 1934, meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> in 1920, and on 23 July 1934 interviewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> for three hours for the New Statesman, criticising the lawlessness, class discrimination and state violence he saw. His The Rights of Man (1940) laid groundwork for the 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> died on 13 August 1946, aged 79, at his home at 13 Hanover Terrace, overlooking Regent's Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He was cremated at Golders Green Crematorium and his ashes were scattered into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> at Old Harry Rocks. Most of his best-known fiction came before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-orwell">George Orwell</a> described him as &quot;too sane to understand the modern world&quot;, while also writing that the world would be perceptibly different had Wells never existed.</p><p>The science fiction historian John Clute calls him the most important writer the genre has yet seen. Impact craters on Mars and the Moon are named after him, and in 1954 the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign bought his literary papers, the largest collection of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, among them The War of the Worlds and The Time Machine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “H. G. Wells” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In ceremonial monarchies, which writers such as <b>H. G. Wells</b> called “crowned republics”, among them the United Kingdom and the other Commonwealth realms, the Netherlands, the Scandinavian kingdoms, Spain and Japan, the monarch has little or no personal discretion.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>H. G. Wells</b> was heavily influenced by Darwinist thought but reacted against social Darwinism, while Jack London wrote survival stories that incorporated his views on it.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>CBS aired Orson Welles's radio adaptation of <b>H. G. Wells</b>'s novel as a series of mock news bulletins about a Martian landing in New Jersey.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There can be no peace now, we realize, but a common peace in all the world; no prosperity but a general prosperity.&#8221;</p><footer>The Outline of History (1920), introduction &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45368/pg45368-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A sense of history as the common adventure of all mankind is as necessary for peace within as it is for peace between the nations.&#8221;</p><footer>The Outline of History (1920), introduction &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45368/pg45368-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
